{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-x{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-1(1)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8638cd63ef4d2773048ae1e27c319a9e35ab2480554279a330002f5aa9f9404c"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x\\sqrt{y}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x\\sqrt{y}+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x\\sqrt{y}+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8638cd63ef4d2773048ae1e27c319a9e35ab2480554279a330002f5aa9f9404c"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-2x\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-x\\sqrt{y}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-2\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2\\sqrt{y}+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+1+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}-1+1+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8638cd63ef4d2773048ae1e27c319a9e35ab2480554279a330002f5aa9f9404c"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-{y}^{3})+({x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.8 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.771\\,429 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.770\\,917\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.77,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8638cd63ef4d2773048ae1e27c319a9e35ab2480554279a330002f5aa9f9404c"} {"question" "q-334450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334450" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.77"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1-x{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((1-x{y}^{2})+(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1+0.5x{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1+0.5x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5(1)({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1+0.5(0)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.25 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.236\\,111 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.236\\,068\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.24,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8638cd63ef4d2773048ae1e27c319a9e35ab2480554279a330002f5aa9f9404c"} {"question" "q-334470" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-334470" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/state.json" "file_sha256" "9d4048e88930a83d503b4b90faa9804bf5c56b2dd1b33a64a0d8011cd4b54d3f"} {"question" "q-333428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071464/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "661c5c81185a4ce4212d7b178d33f84365140aeb86567b2136f04b3da081e608" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "The trapezoidal update is Y=y+(h/2)[f(x,y)+f(x+h,Y)]. With x=y=h=1 and f(x,y)=2xy², this becomes Y=1+(1/2)[2+4Y²]=2+2Y². Moving terms left yields g(Y)=Y-2Y²-2, exactly original option 3. The authentic worked solution derives this expression and the screenshot grades that option Correct. The stored expression has the same function argument, coefficients, power and signs; only subscript formatting differs. The question asks for the residual function, whose derivation remains valid even though this step has no real root." "bindings" {"problem" "e1db2f06ab8cfae9dc7976cebf61dfb5b46f7c7fa6ec77e1b490bca71454a5f1" "worked_solution" "e36124ea0412979dd4ad136677738fd720d3c368c42e118e68f61ada1a2396ea" "original_field" "855ccd15f41a0b35a84282f488c693b0d86ab6299221f35eb3c57ecf783a60ec" "reviewed_field" "7b12327f38d4e76db0d575af01de8961e341a79a7a2d577ef86c039b3a3bc7bc" "raw_widget" "e286c205e765277fe0abfa1009c0008bab981d036c66a2cb8194f1bf207c9426"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-333428-before.json" "sha256" "af2bb08f020470e83a33a3baf9b95e5a95e4695fae4b22d439ad32785441d738"} {"path" "q-333428-after.png" "sha256" "ef6bec8fe69ec9b9ed74f84bcca80311629c8354ce10fc489e00916c0319db2e"}]} {"question" "q-333428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-4"]] "cat